I reviewed the new head as well, including the just-pushed `fbd8d071…`. The direction is right, but I would **block Episode Zero until the core invariants below are proven by tests**.

The newest commit fixes `max_contestants → max_appearances` and removes global comedian-name uniqueness, but those are surface fixes.  The bigger problem is that the codebase currently contains **multiple generations of the architecture at once**.

### Peer-review verdict

**P0 — The Python HTTP routes do not match the canonical domain model.** The canonical model correctly says `User → Comedian → ActVersion → Submission → Appearance`: `Comedian` requires `owner_user_id`; `ActVersion` uses `revision`, `manifest`, `created_by_user_id`, `content_sha256`; and `Appearance` is created only from a selected `Submission`.  But the current comedian route still creates `Comedian` without an owner, accepts `creator_id` from the browser, creates `ActVersion(version=..., minute_text=..., content_hash=...)`, and truncates hashes to 16 hex characters. Those fields no longer correspond to the canonical model.

The episode route has the same problem. It still creates an `Appearance` when someone merely enters the show, queries `Appearance.comedian_id`, passes `comedian_id` and `act_version_id` into `Appearance`, and then uses `random.shuffle()` over appearances. The canonical `Appearance` has none of those ownership fields and should exist **only after a Submission has won the draw**.

So `Submission → deterministic draw → Appearance` exists conceptually, but **the actual public route bypasses it**.

**P0 — the migration describes an older application.** `001_initial_schema.py` creates the old `episode_contestants` model, old ActVersion columns, globally unique comedian names, `max_contestants`, and no canonical `users → submissions → appearances` structure.  Running `alembic upgrade head` therefore does not prove the current application works; it creates a database for a previous version of it.

**P0 — authentication is not safe yet.** The Cloudflare authentication middleware explicitly *decodes* a Privy JWT without verifying its cryptographic signature. A forged token with a chosen `sub` can therefore be treated as authenticated.  On the Python side, the admin routes themselves contain no auth dependency at all.  The current integration test obscures this by accepting either `401` **or** `422`, so an invalid UUID can make the “admin requires auth” test pass without auth ever running.

**P0 — the live Worker is not yet durable or access-controlled.** `EpisodeRoom` determines whether a socket is `stage`, `puppeteer`, or `audience` solely from a query parameter. It doesn't validate a scoped token. Its in-memory `episodeState` isn't restored from storage after object recreation, missed events aren't replayed, puppeteer speech increments the event sequence without persisting the event, and the crowd's “unique laughers” set uses `clientSeq`, meaning one user can generate unlimited “unique” laughers simply by incrementing their counter.

**P0 — payments must remain disabled.** The crypto `confirm_tip()` marks a transaction confirmed from caller-supplied `tx_hash` and amount without verifying the chain transaction, mint, destinations or amount.  The Stripe service similarly has a `confirm_tip()` that trusts supplied values, and its webhook handler explicitly does not verify Stripe signatures.  These are useful interfaces/scaffolds, but they are not payment implementations yet.

**P0 — the Cloudflare runtime isn't buildable from a clean checkout yet.** The latest tree has the Worker source and `wrangler.jsonc`, but no `package.json`, lockfile, `tsconfig`, Vite configuration or React application.  The Worker API also imports `requireAuth` even though the auth module doesn't export it and uses `requireAdmin` without importing it.  `wrangler.jsonc` also lacks a Durable Object binding/migration despite `EpisodeRoom` existing.

There are some important P1s too. `get_next_seq()` uses `MAX(seq)+1`; that is **not concurrency-safe** despite the docstring saying it guarantees uniqueness. Two transactions can read the same maximum.  API-key `check_scope()` simply returns `True`, so scopes currently do nothing, while its in-memory rate limiter calls `datetime.replace(timestamp=...)`, which isn't a valid `datetime.replace` argument.

The BYO-agent implementation is premature and creates an SSRF surface because it will POST to arbitrary user-controlled URLs. Tokens are also only stored in process memory.  I would feature-flag this completely off until after Episode Zero.

There are also correctness bugs in media. The performance compiler replaces `?` with `.` before checking whether a sentence contains `?`, so its question branch can never fire; `max_segment_ms` is unused; timing is spread evenly across sentences rather than using actual speech alignment; and short audio can yield a negative exit cue.  The TTS implementation writes local files, permits arbitrary raw voice names, guesses MP3 duration from file size, and doesn't actually reuse an existing cached file.

Finally, the new problem registry is useful, but **do not treat it as ground truth yet**. It marks truncated Act hashes as resolved, while the current comedian route still returns `hexdigest()[:16]`. It marks event sequencing resolved despite `MAX(seq)+1` remaining race-prone, and it does not include the unverified JWT, payment verification, SSRF, migration/model incompatibility or unauthenticated Python admin routes.

And the current “tests passing” number is misleading. The suite includes assertions such as “201 or 401 or 422,” “200 or 404,” and “101 or 400 or 404,” while event sequencing tests merely check that functions are callable.   Those tests prevent almost no regressions.

